From da2e278c1117604793a4209eaa832b16834f9d7c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:01:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] nfc: do not wait forever for a proxy that never starts

g_subprocess_launcher_take_fd leaves the launcher holding its own copy of
the fd until it is finalized, and the launcher here is a g_autoptr that
outlives the read below it. So the portal was holding the proxy's end of
the sync socket itself, and the read for the readiness byte could never see
EOF: a proxy that died before signalling ready -- a rejected argument, an
LSM denial, a socket it could not bind -- hung that read forever, on
xdg-desktop-portal's single main loop, wedging every portal in the session.
Close the launcher's copy as soon as the child is spawned.

Cap the proxies one sender may hold open. Each costs the portal a file
descriptor and a process, so an application looping on OpenNFCRemote
exhausts xdg-desktop-portal rather than itself.

Put the portal behind a meson feature option, so that xdg-dbus-proxy is a
dependency for those who build NFC support and nobody else, the way geoclue
gates the location portal and gudev gates USB.

Also: unlink the socket before removing the directory on the failure path,
since a proxy that bound it and exited does not clean up after itself; scope
both broadcast rules to a path, so that every rule in the filter is scoped;
and say in the comment that the socket is only unreachable after the unlink,
rather than claiming it was never reachable at all.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
