From 64db92e39524612d8a271e2cb85e238ee510320c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 21:36:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] nfc: bound the readiness wait, and say what the interface
 means

The wait for xdg-dbus-proxy's readiness byte covered only half of what can
go wrong. A proxy that *dies* is caught by the read seeing EOF, once the
parent has dropped its copy of the write end. A proxy that *starts and
hangs* -- blocked connecting to or authenticating against an unresponsive
system bus -- writes no byte and never exits, so there is no EOF to see and
the read never returns: the call, the thread it runs on and the process it
spawned are held for the life of the portal, and nothing will ever reap any
of them. poll() with a timeout, and give up.

Giving up also has to kill the child. Dropping the last reference to a
GSubprocess does not, so both failure paths now say so explicitly rather
than leaving a proxy running with nothing holding it.

The rest is what a first read of this interface from outside would ask for:

  - OpenNFCRemote gets org.gtk.GDBus.C.Name, so gdbus-codegen emits
    handle_open_nfc_remote rather than handle_open_nfcremote, the way
    Camera's OpenPipeWireRemote already does;
  - both methods get org.qtproject.QtDBus.QtTypeName.In0, which every other
    portal XML with an a{sv} in-arg carries and this one did not, and which
    Qt and KDE generate their bindings from;
  - the XML says that the returned connection's lifetime is tied to the
    caller's session bus connection and not to the fd. That is a real
    difference from OpenPipeWireRemote, it is what an application author
    will assume it shares, and getting it wrong looks exactly like the
    connection dying at random a fifth of a second in;
  - the permission constants move to xdp-types.h beside CAMERA_'s, with an
    NFC_DBUS_IFACE to match, so this is no longer the one file in src/
    defining its own;
  - the file carries the full licence block the other data/*.xml do, not
    just the SPDX line;
  - a NEWS entry.

The test for a proxy that hangs needs a stand-in that starts and stays,
which /bin/false cannot be. It also asserts the process is gone afterwards,
which is the half that catches a missing force_exit.

And xdg_dbus_proxy_pids() skips on a kernel without CONFIG_PROC_CHILDREN
instead of raising FileNotFoundError, which read as two unrelated failures.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
