From 2a99fd83162e1073be4f9d0c02080c20ff1dc002 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 21:56:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] nfc: the proxy table is reached from two threads, so lock
 it

nfc->proxies was an unlocked GHashTable mutated from more than one thread.
xdp_context_take_and_export_portal exports every portal that is not the
host portal with HANDLE_METHOD_INVOCATIONS_IN_THREAD, so OpenNFCRemote --
its count of a sender's proxies, its insert, and its rollback -- runs on a
GTask worker thread, while the two paths that free a slot, a proxy exiting
and the caller leaving the bus, run on a main context. Two applications
calling OpenNFCRemote at the same instant is a concurrent insert during a
possible resize, and an iteration concurrent with an insert.

Guard every access with a G_LOCK, which is what xdp-session.c does with its
own table. Two things shape where the critical sections fall.

The lock is not held across anything that blocks. nfc_proxy_new spawns a
process and then waits up to five seconds for it to signal readiness;
holding a global lock across that would put every other caller behind the
slowest spawn and turn a fixed cost into a queue. So the spawn happens
between two critical sections, not inside one.

That leaves the count and the insert in different critical sections, which
is where the cap would have leaked: a burst of simultaneous calls could
each pass the check and then all insert. The insert now re-counts under the
same lock it inserts with, so the cap is exact, and a call that loses the
race gives back the proxy it had already started. The cheap check before
the spawn stays, so an application looping on this does not pay to start a
process it will not be given.

Nothing called while the lock is held takes it again. The one thing that
runs under the lock and is not a table operation is nfc_proxy_free, as the
table's key-destroy function: it closes the sync fd, which makes
xdg-dbus-proxy exit, but the child watch that notices only dispatches from
a main context once the current callback has returned, and the reference it
drops to the GSubprocess is never the last one while a wait is pending.
Arming that wait moves inside the insert's critical section, because from
the moment the table holds the proxy a peer disconnect on the main context
can free it, and proxy->subprocess with it.

The two lifecycle sites stay unlocked and say why: the table is created in
nfc_new before the portal is exported, and destroyed in finalize once
nothing holds a reference to the Nfc.

Also rewrap a paragraph in the XML that had a sentence starting mid-line.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
