From 22e4ead291590ed348d9c9aa6099011c32b9ccb0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 10:18:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] nfc: revoking the permission closes connections already
 open

Turning an application off under Privacy > NFC only made its next
OpenNFCRemote fail. A process that had asked before the switch was
turned off kept the filtered connection it already held, and went on
reading tags for as long as it liked -- which is not what someone
turning that switch off means. Observed on a phone: the application
showed the radio's state and read a card while its stored permission
said no.

Watch the permission store for changes to devices/nfc and close the
proxies belonging to any application that no longer has a yes. Closing
the proxy closes its socket, so the application sees its connection drop
and can say so.

The Changed signal carries the whole table, so no round trip is needed
to decide, and an application with no entry at all has had its
permission deleted, which is not a yes either.
---
 src/nfc.c | 98 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 98 insertions(+)

